# Backend Services

Services talk to the backend. Hooks and Server Components consume services — never call `fetch` directly in UI code.

## Layer Responsibilities

| Layer | Location | Does | Does not |
|-------|----------|------|----------|
| HTTP client | `lib/api/client.ts` | Base URL, headers, JSON parse, error mapping | Domain logic |
| Service | `lib/services/*.ts` | One resource/domain, typed request/response | React state, JSX, `useEffect` |
| Hook | `hooks/use*.ts` | Loading/error state, refetch, client-side calls | Raw `fetch`, URL strings |
| Server Component | `app/**` | Import service directly, pass data as props | `'use client'` for data fetch |

## Service Rules

- **One domain per file** — `order.service.ts`, not `api.ts` with everything
- **Named async functions** — `getOrder`, `createOrder`; no classes, no singletons
- **Typed I/O** — import request/response interfaces from `@/types/*` (see typescript-global-types), not inline in the service file
- **Throw on failure** — return data on success; let hooks/components decide how to show errors
- **Same paths as MSW** — service URLs must match handlers in `mocks/handlers/`

```typescript
// lib/services/health.service.ts
import { apiClient } from "@/lib/api/client";
import type { HealthResponse } from "@/types/health";

export async function getHealth(): Promise<HealthResponse> {
  return apiClient.get<HealthResponse>("/api/health");
}
```

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — React in a service
export function useHealth() {
  const [data, setData] = useState(null);
  useEffect(() => { fetch("/api/health")... }, []);
}

// ❌ BAD — fetch scattered in a hook
export function useOrder(id: string) {
  useEffect(() => {
    fetch(`/api/orders/${id}`).then((r) => r.json()).then(setOrder);
  }, [id]);
}

// ✅ GOOD — hook delegates to service
import { getOrder } from "@/lib/services/order.service";

export function useOrder(id: string) {
  const [order, setOrder] = useState<Order | null>(null);
  const [isLoading, setIsLoading] = useState(true);
  const [error, setError] = useState<Error | null>(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    let cancelled = false;
    setIsLoading(true);
    getOrder(id)
      .then((data) => { if (!cancelled) setOrder(data); })
      .catch((err) => { if (!cancelled) setError(err); })
      .finally(() => { if (!cancelled) setIsLoading(false); });
    return () => { cancelled = true; };
  }, [id]);

  return { order, isLoading, error };
}
```

## Keep It Simple

- No premature abstractions — a plain function per endpoint beats a generic CRUD factory
- No caching in services — cache at the Server Component (`'use cache'`) or hook level if needed
- Extract a shared client only when the second service appears; until then, one small helper is fine
- Server Components: `const orders = await getOrders()` — no hook required
